      "## Intersectionality and within-group differences",
      "Clients hold multiple identities and positions that interact. A policy may affect two people who share one identity differently because of disability, language, class, immigration experience, geography, age, gender, religion, or another context.",
      "Avoid:",
      "- Treating one person as representative of a group\n- Assuming marginalized identity means political agreement\n- Reducing the client to oppression while overlooking agency and strengths\n- Assuming privilege eliminates suffering\n- Using demographic categories without asking how the client understands them",
